            "name": "Fairfax Station"
          },
          "description": "Scaling and root planing at Livewell Dental is a non-surgical deep cleaning for gum disease that removes plaque, tartar, and bacteria below the gumline and smooths root surfaces to support healing.",
          "name": "Scaling & Root Planing",
          "provider": {
            "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/#organization"
          },
          "serviceType": "Scaling and root planing",
          "url": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/services/scaling-root-planing"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/services/scaling-root-planing#faq",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "isPartOf": {
            "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/services/scaling-root-planing#webpage"
          },
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A routine cleaning removes plaque and tartar from the surfaces of your teeth above and just at the gumline. Scaling and root planing goes deeper. It removes bacterial buildup from below the gumline and along the roots, where a regular cleaning cannot reach. It is prescribed when gum disease has already progressed and pockets have formed between your teeth and gums."
              },
              "name": "How is scaling and root planing different from a regular cleaning?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "For most patients, yes. Once the bacterial buildup is removed and root surfaces are smooth, gum tissue can begin to reattach and inflammation can resolve. Healing depends on the severity of the disease, your home care habits, and whether you return for follow-up visits. Some patients see significant improvement. Others with advanced disease may need additional treatment."
              },
              "name": "Will my gums heal after treatment?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The procedure is done under local anesthesia, so you should not feel pain during treatment. Some sensitivity and mild soreness for a few days after is normal as tissue heals. Most patients manage this with over-the-counter pain relief. If you experience sensitivity to cold after treatment, it usually resolves within a few weeks."
              },
              "name": "Is scaling and root planing painful?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Most cases are completed in two visits, treating one side of the mouth per appointment. This allows for proper anesthesia and gives treated tissue time to begin healing before the second side is addressed. A follow-up evaluation is typically scheduled four to eight weeks later to assess how the gums have responded."
              },
              "name": "How many visits does scaling and root planing take?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes. Scaling and root planing treats the current infection but does not prevent future gum disease. Consistent home care, regular professional cleanings (usually every three to four months for the first year), and ongoing monitoring are essential. Patients who maintain consistent follow-up care have the best long-term outcomes."
              },
              "name": "Can gum disease come back after treatment?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "If gum disease has been diagnosed, yes. Gum disease does not resolve on its own. Without treatment, it progresses. Bone loss and attachment loss are permanent. Teeth can loosen and eventually be lost. Gum disease has also been linked to heart disease and diabetes. Treating it early is significantly less invasive and less costly than treating advanced disease."
              },
              "name": "Is scaling and root planing really necessary?"
            }
          ]
